This file's presence, not its contents, is the whole message: s6-supervise
starts a service whose directory contains a file called `down` in the DOWN
state instead of starting it. s6 never opens the file, so the reasoning can
live in it rather than in a comment somewhere else that would drift away from
it. (The gate checks that too, with this text in place, because a fact this
file depends on is worth measuring rather than believing.)

WHY NGINX SHIPS DOWN. It is ORDERING. s6-svscan starts a service the instant
it notices the directory, which is a fraction of a second after init.d/S40s6
runs -- before init.d/S60nginx has checked that the nginx binary is where it
thinks it is, before its config has been put in place, and before moonraker
is coming up behind it. A web server that binds :80 at that moment serves
Mainsail a backend that does not exist yet, and the first thing a user sees is
a UI blaming Klipper for something nginx did.

This used to be argued from a switch, MOD_WEB, which no longer exists. The
argument was never really about the switch: it was that THE SCANNER DOES NOT
DECIDE WHEN NGINX STARTS. init.d/S60nginx does, at the point in the boot where
its prerequisites have been checked, by calling svc_s6_up -- which overrides
this file for as long as that s6-supervise lives.

THE COST, stated honestly, because it is the one thing this arrangement gives
up. `down` is consulted when s6-supervise STARTS, not when the service dies:
once S60nginx has brought nginx up, s6 keeps restarting it on every crash for
as long as that supervisor lives, which is the entire point of the migration
and is unaffected by this file. What it does mean is that if s6-supervise
ITSELF is killed -- not nginx, the supervisor -- s6-svscan starts a fresh one,
that fresh one reads this file, and nginx stays down until something runs
`S60nginx start` again. That is a rare failure (s6-supervise is a small C
program that blocks in poll() and is not the thing that crashes) with an
honest signal (`S60nginx status` says it is not running, and says so from
s6-svstat rather than from a pidfile) and a one-line fix from an ssh session.
The alternative -- deleting this file at runtime once the boot has got far
enough -- buys back that corner case at the price of a payload that rewrites
its own install tree, which every path in this mod is arranged not to do.

This file is not a record of anything having gone wrong: it is the state nginx
ships in, on every build, and nothing removes it.
